    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Stop if you feel sharp, radiating or nerve-type pain.",
        "Muscle glow colours and activation curves are illustrative, not measured EMG.",
        "Low-back pain, disc problems or flexion intolerance: start with a McGill-style curl-up, dead bug or plank; partial curl-ups gave the best abdominal challenge for the least spine compression in one model study (Axler and McGill 1997).",
        "Hip-flexor heavy variants (inclined reverse crunch) gave high rectus femoris activity, which may not suit some people with low-back problems (Escamilla 2006).",
        "Pregnancy and postpartum (diastasis recti): get individual guidance before dynamic trunk-flexion or straight-leg work."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Low-back discomfort: smaller curl (tailbone just off the mat) or a posterior pelvic tilt with feet on the floor.",
        "Neck tension: relax the head on the mat."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Posterior pelvic tilt with feet on the floor.",
        "Dead bug.",
        "Reverse crunch with feet tapping the floor between reps."
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Slow 3 s lowering.",
        "Decline-bench reverse crunch.",
        "Hanging knee raise."
      ]
    },
    "muscleNotes": {
      "core": "Rectus abdominis curls the pelvis toward the ribs. A reverse crunch on a 30-degree incline was among the highest upper and lower rectus activations tested (Escamilla 2006); on the flat floor a reverse curl-up gave less rectus activity than a curl-up (Clark 2003). A posterior pelvic tilt worked the lower rectus harder than a curl-up in trained people (Sarti 1996).",
      "obliques_r": "Internal obliques were highly active in the inclined reverse crunch (Escamilla 2006); the obliques also steady the pelvis as it rolls.",
      "obliques_l": "Internal obliques were highly active in the inclined reverse crunch (Escamilla 2006); the obliques also steady the pelvis as it rolls.",
      "hip_flexors_r": "Hold the thighs up at about 90 degrees and bring the knees in; they should not swing the legs. The aim is the pelvis curl, not more hip flexion.",
      "hip_flexors_l": "Hold the thighs up at about 90 degrees and bring the knees in; they should not swing the legs. The aim is the pelvis curl, not more hip flexion.",
      "quads_r": "Rectus femoris works as a hip flexor: its activity was high in the inclined reverse crunch (Escamilla 2006), which the authors flagged for people with low-back problems.",
      "quads_l": "Rectus femoris works as a hip flexor: its activity was high in the inclined reverse crunch (Escamilla 2006), which the authors flagged for people with low-back problems."
    }
  },
